# Slide Deck Outline — Build Your First AI Prototype

> **Source of truth:** This file now documents the actual, final built deck (`Build the Framework First.dc.html` in Claude Design, project "Build AI Prototype"). It was reverse-synced from the live deck on 2026-07-08, so wording and structure here should match what's on screen exactly.
> `ai-prototype-development-lesson.md` remains the source of truth for teaching content, timing, and instructor notes. If the two ever disagree going forward, treat the live deck as the presentation reality and flag the lesson file for reconciliation rather than silently trusting either one.
> This file contains slide-specific concerns: layout, visual content, kickers, and structure — matched to the real deck.

---

## Prompt to use

```
Using the outline in this file, update the HTML slide deck for Build Your First AI Prototype.

Follow the rules in _System/rules/SLIDE_DECK_RULES.md exactly.
File: Library/lessons/ai-prototype-development/materials/Build the Framework First.dc.html
Copy tokens.css and deck-stage.js locally into Library/lessons/ai-prototype-development/ so the deck is self-contained.

VISUAL DESIGN DIRECTION — apply globally to every slide:

- Prefer diagrams, flows, and visual metaphors over bullet lists. If content can be shown
  as a shape, flow, or diagram — do that instead of listing text.
- Use inline SVG for all diagrams. Keep them flat, clean, and minimal —
  use only token colours (--sienna, --ochre, --sage, --ink, --paper) plus the deck's purple/violet accent.
- Milestone/activity slides (.milestone) should feel like a full-bleed pause moment — no extra
  content, just the kicker, timer, and the one thing students are doing.
- Every slide should have one dominant visual or diagram. If the layout is mostly text,
  add a supporting SVG shape or icon to anchor the eye.
- Avoid centred bullet lists. Use .process or .numbered layouts with visual numbering.
```

