### STATEMENT — Celebrate what you built
- Kicker: Look what you made
- Title: Last session, you built something *real.*
- Lead: That's a complete design system, and you built it. Most people skip this step and start prototyping from nothing. You've got a head start.
- Speaker notes: Before we go anywhere, take a moment. Last session you built something real. You didn't skip the foundation work. Tokens. Atoms. Molecules. Organisms. A template. A page. Most people jump straight to prototyping with a blank file and no system. You have a system. That is the thing that makes everything today possible. Say it, let it land, move on.
- 🎨 Visual: Plain text slide, no diagram — "real." set in the accent purple. Deliberately restrained; the point is the pause, not the visual.

---

### STATEMENT — The bridge from last session
- Kicker: So… what now?
- Title: From your screens, to a system, to something that *runs.*
- Lead: Your key screens already hold the system. Pull it out, hand it to AI, and get back a prototype that moves.
- Speaker notes: Here's the reframe. Your design system is not the finish line. It's the starting gun. You made every visual decision that matters. Today we fire the gun. The system you built last session is the input. AI reads that input and builds the prototype. The question for today: how do you make that handoff as clean as possible?
- 🎨 Visual: Plain text slide, no diagram. "Something that runs" set in accent purple.

---

### STATEMENT — What is an AI-assisted prototype?
- Kicker: Let's be specific
- Title: Not a Figma file. Not a mockup. *A prototype that actually runs.*
- Lead: Real code running in a browser, not a picture of an app. You define the system once; AI assembles the screens from it.
- Speaker notes: Let's be precise about what we're building. An AI-assisted prototype is not a Figma mockup. It's real code running in a browser. You define the rules: what components exist, what states they can have, how screens connect. The AI reads those rules and assembles the screens from them. Your job is to define the system clearly enough that AI doesn't have to guess. That's the whole skill.
- 🎨 Visual: Right side, vertical flow diagram of 3 connected boxes with arrows: "Prototype pattern" (yellow outline) → "AI" (solid purple) → phone-outline box (purple outline, showing UI bars inside) labelled "Running in browser." Each box has a side label: "You define this" / "AI assembles" / "You review."

---

### DIAGRAM — Traditional vs AI-assisted prototype
- Kicker: Why this session exists
- Title: Traditional prototype vs *AI-assisted prototype.*
- 2-column comparison:
  - **Traditional prototype** (cream/neutral card) — "Every screen by hand." You design each screen manually. Every state, every interaction, every component placed one at a time. Changes require updating every screen individually. AI can't build from it. There's no single source of truth. Scales to 3 screens. Falls apart at 10.
  - **AI-assisted prototype** (purple-tinted card, highlighted) — "Define once. Build many." You define the prototype pattern once: what components exist (with states) and how screens connect. AI reads the prototype pattern and generates every screen from it. Changes update from the pattern, not from each individual screen. Scales to 30 screens as easily as 3.
- Speaker notes: Here's what's actually different. Traditional prototyping means designing every screen by hand. Screen by screen, element by element. It works for three screens. It falls apart at ten. AI-assisted flips the process: you define the system once, and AI assembles the screens from it. You stop being the assembler. You become the architect.
- 🎨 Visual: Two side-by-side cards, "TRADITIONAL PROTOTYPE" label in muted orange on the left card, "AI-ASSISTED PROTOTYPE" label in purple on the right (highlighted) card with a purple border.

