## 2. The problem with screen-by-screen

---

### SECTION 02
- Title: The problem with\nscreen-by-screen
- Sub: Why starting with a screen is the wrong move
- 🎨 Visual: Dark (--ink) background, faint giant "02" numeral top-left, title in white, centred.

---

### STATEMENT — What goes wrong
- Kicker: The most common mistake
- Title: Screen by screen. *Everything drifts.*
- Lead: Home, then detail, then settings. By screen three the button has drifted and the spacing has moved. Hand that to AI and every prompt starts from zero, because nothing connects the screens.
- Speaker notes: Here's what usually happens. You open Figma and start with the home screen. Then the detail screen. Then settings. By screen three, the button looks slightly different, the spacing changed. There's a component that isn't in your system. When you hand it to AI, every prompt starts from zero, because nothing connects the screens. Don't rush this slide; let them recognise themselves in it.
- 🎨 Visual: Three phone-frame outlines drifting apart left to right, each subtly different (rounded corners vary slightly, button size varies). Callout labels with dotted leader lines: "Spacing changed," "Different radius," "Not in system." Frames labelled underneath: Screen 1, Screen 2, Screen 3.

---

## 3. The pattern-first solution

---

### SECTION 03
- Title: Get everything\nready before hand
- Sub: The pattern-first solution
- 🎨 Visual: Solid purple/violet background, faint giant "03" numeral top-left, title in white, centred.

---

### STATEMENT — Define the system, screens assemble themselves
- Kicker: Here's the trick
- Title: Define the system. *Screens assemble themselves.*
- Lead: Define your components, connections, and states before any screen.
- Speaker notes: So we flip it. Before you touch a single screen, define three things: what components exist, how screens connect, and what states each component has. Once that framework exists, every screen is just an arrangement of things that already exist. Left: drift, loose frames pulling apart. Right: same three screens snapping into place from shared blocks. AI doesn't start from zero anymore.
- 🎨 Visual: Two-column diagram. Left, labelled "SCREEN BY SCREEN" — three loose, disconnected phone-frame outlines, unaligned. Right, labelled "PATTERN FIRST" — four small coloured blocks at top (purple shades) feeding down through a shared branching line into three aligned, bordered phone frames.

---

### STATEMENT — What is a prototype pattern?
- Kicker: Let's break it down
- Title: A prototype pattern is just a really clear set *of instructions.*
- Lead: Think of it like a recipe. You don't start cooking and figure out the ingredients as you go — you list everything first. A prototype pattern lists your components, your screens, and your states before any building starts.
- Speaker notes: A prototype pattern is not a scary technical thing. It's just a really clear set of instructions. Think of it like a recipe. You don't start cooking and figure out the ingredients as you go. You list everything first. A prototype framework is the same: it lists your components, your screens, and your states before any building starts. AI loves a good recipe.
- 🎨 Visual: Left, a card titled "Ingredients" with a bulleted list — Components: buttons, inputs, cards, nav; Screens: what they are, how they connect; States: what each component can look like. Right, an arrow labelled "AI FOLLOWS THE RECIPE" pointing to a bordered phone-frame icon showing UI bars and a filled button, labelled "Running in browser."

---

### DIAGRAM — Layer 1: Component inventory
- Kicker: The first layer
- Title: What pieces do you have, and what can they *look like?*
- Lead: The component inventory: every UI piece in your system, with all its states listed right alongside it.
- Speaker notes: The first layer is the component inventory: every UI piece in your system, with all its states included right alongside it. Default, hover, loading, disabled, error: all of it lives here, inside the component entry. Not in a separate layer. Think of it like telling LEGO which bricks are in the box, and what colour each brick can be.
- Progress nav shown at left (used consistently across this slide and the next): "Component inventory" (active, filled purple dot) → "Interaction pattern" (upcoming, grey dot).
- 🎨 Visual: Four bordered cards (yellow outline) stacked: Button (default · hover · loading · disabled), Input (default · filled · error · success), Card (default · hover · active · selected), "Nav bar · Avatar · …". A dark callout card to the right reads "States live inside the component, not a separate layer" with the second half in accent yellow/orange.

---

### DIAGRAM — Layer 2: Interaction pattern
- Kicker: The second layer
- Title: Where does each screen *lead?*
- Lead: A diagram of your screens and the paths between them, and what triggers each move.
- Speaker notes: The second layer is the interaction pattern: a diagram of your screens and the paths between them. Screen A → tap button → Screen B. Under what condition? What triggers the move? This is the map AI follows when it builds navigation. The component inventory is the bricks. The interaction pattern is the floor plan.
- Progress nav: same as Layer 1 slide, but now "Component inventory" shows complete (checkmark, dimmed) and "Interaction pattern" is the active/highlighted step — fixed order preserved between slides so the nav reads as one continuous progression, not a reshuffle.
- 🎨 Visual: Three bordered phone-frame outlines (yellow) left to right — "Home" → arrow labelled "Tap sign up" → "Sign up form" → arrow labelled "Submit → success" → "Dashboard." Each frame shows placeholder UI bars/blocks and a filled button or circle.

---

### DIAGRAM — Interaction pattern types
- Kicker: Every screen is doing one of these
- Title: Read. Edit. Add. Confirm. *And a few others.*
- Lead: Most prototypes use 3–4 types. Label each screen before you start building.
- Speaker notes: Every screen in your prototype is doing one of a small number of things. Knowing the type tells AI what components it needs, what states to include, and what transitions to build. Before you start Phase 4, label each screen in your prototype pattern with its type. Most student prototypes use 3–4 types.
- 7 types, two rows of cards:
  - **Read** — User views content, no state change. Dashboard · feed · profile.
  - **Edit** — User modifies existing content. Edit profile · settings.
  - **Add** — User creates something new. New post · add to cart.
  - **Confirm** — User reviews and approves an action. Order summary · delete.
  - **Navigate** — User moves between sections. Tab bar · side menu.
  - **Search** — User queries or narrows a list. Results · filter · sort.
  - **Onboard** — User guided through first-time setup. Welcome · tutorial steps · permissions.
- 🎨 Visual: Seven neutral cards in a 4+3 grid, each with bold type name, one-line description, and small grey example-screen text underneath. Footer line in italic: "Most prototypes use 3–4 types. Label each screen before you start building."

---

### STATEMENT — Two layers, one document
- Kicker: Put it all together
- Title: Two layers. One document. *AI builds from it.*
- Lead: Component inventory (with states inside) + Interaction pattern = your prototype pattern.
- Speaker notes: Put it all together. Component inventory: every piece with all its states already inside, plus the interaction pattern. That's your prototype pattern. Two layers. One document. This is what you hand to AI, or what AI reads from your codebase. The better this document is, the more accurate the output. You built most of this in the last session. Today you make it explicit.
- 🎨 Visual: Two stacked full-width cards — "Component inventory" (pale yellow, subtext "states included: default · hover · loading · error · success") above "Interaction pattern" (solid gold/yellow, subtext "how screens connect, and under what conditions") — with a bold black arrow pointing right to a bordered phone-frame icon (purple outline, showing UI bars and a filled toggle/button).

