## 4. Build

---

### SECTION 04
- Title: Build
- Sub: Pattern in hand. Time to make something real.
- 🎨 Visual: Dark (--ink) background, faint giant "04" numeral top-left, title in white, centred.

---

### PROCESS — Step 1 of 6: Clean up your design file  *(⚠️ not yet built — see note below)*
- Kicker: Step 1 of 6
- Title: Clean up your design file *first.*
- Lead:
  - Delete or archive components you're not using in this prototype
  - Merge duplicate variants of the same component
  - Remove hidden/test layers and old exploration frames
  - Confirm naming is consistent across the file
- Prompt to use (for large files): "Here is my Figma component list: [paste]. Which of these look like duplicates, unused variants, or one-off experiments rather than real system components?"
- Speaker notes: Step 1. Before AI reads anything, remove what shouldn't be there. AI can't tell "this is part of my system" from "I forgot to delete this" — it will include all of it in the component inventory, and the prototype ends up bloated with things nobody meant to ship. This feels like tidying, not real work. It isn't optional.
- 🎨 Visual: Suggest a "before/after" pair — left, a cluttered component panel with greyed-out duplicate and unused entries crossed out; right, the same panel cleaned down to only the components in use. Match the visual language of the Step 3/4 component-panel mockups already in the deck.
- ⚠️ **This step does not exist in the live deck yet.** Added to the lesson file after a mentee pattern showed up repeatedly: students skip cleaning their Figma file, so the AI-generated component inventory (Step 4) inherits every unused/duplicate component along with it, and the prototype gets built on top of the mess instead of the system. Insert as the new Step 1, and renumber the existing Step 1–5 slides below to Step 2–6 (already reflected in the headings below). Note: the lesson file no longer tracks per-phase/per-step durations at all — timing language was intentionally removed throughout, so don't reintroduce minute counts when this slide is built.

---

### PROCESS — Step 2 of 6: Scope the build
- Kicker: Step 2 of 6
- Title: Scope the build. *Don't build everything.*
- Lead:
  - Pick 2–3 screens from your interaction pattern.
  - The goal is to prove the pattern works, not to finish the prototype.
- Speaker notes: Step 2. Pick 2 to 3 screens from your interaction pattern. Don't try to build everything. The goal is to prove the pattern works, not to finish the prototype.
- 🎨 Visual: Five phone-frame slots in a row. First two are solid purple with placeholder UI content, labelled "Pick these." Remaining three are dashed-outline empty placeholders labelled "Not yet."

---

### PROCESS — Step 3 of 6: Sync your design tokens
- Kicker: Step 3 of 6
- Title: Sync your design tokens as *your design system.*
- Prompt to use: "Read my design file via MCP. Extract the design tokens: colour values, typography styles, and spacing. Generate a CSS variables file from these tokens so every screen uses the exact values from my design system."
- Lead: This runs once — every screen after uses your real tokens, not AI's generic defaults.
- Speaker notes: Step 3. Read the design file via MCP, extract tokens, generate a CSS variables file. This runs once. Every screen after this uses your actual colours and type, not AI defaults. Without this step, AI defaults to generic, interchangeable visual styles. Token sync is what makes the output look like your product.
- 🎨 Visual: Dark prompt card on the left labelled "PROMPT TO USE" with the quote above (colour values / typography styles / spacing highlighted in accent colours). Right: light card showing a colour swatch row (6 grey shades) and a typography scale sample (Display, Heading 1, Heading 2, Heading 3).

---

### PROCESS — Step 4 of 6: Generate the component inventory
- Kicker: Step 4 of 6
- Title: Generate the *component inventory.*
- Prompt to use: "Read my design file. Generate a component inventory: list every component with its name, purpose, and all its states (default, hover, loading, error, etc.). Flag anything that looks incomplete or inconsistent."
- Lead: Review the output. Correct any names or states that are wrong. This becomes the source of truth for everything you build.
- Speaker notes: Step 4. Read the design file, generate a component inventory listing every component with its name, purpose, and all its states. Students review the output and correct names or states that are wrong. This becomes the source of truth for the build. If Step 1 was actually done, this list should be short and clean.
- 🎨 Visual: Dark prompt card on the left (name / purpose / states highlighted). Right: light mockup of a component-library panel showing a "Buttons" component with Contained and Outlined variants, each broken out by colour (Primary/Secondary/Error), state (Enabled/Hover/Disabled), and size (Large/Medium/Small).

---

### PROCESS — Step 5 of 6: Generate the interaction pattern
- Kicker: Step 5 of 6
- Title: Generate the *interaction pattern.*
- Prompt to use: "Based on the component inventory, map the interaction pattern for my prototype: which screens exist, what connects them, and what triggers each transition. Use the component inventory as your reference."
- Lead: Which screens exist, how they connect, and what triggers each move between them.
- Speaker notes: Step 5. Based on the component inventory, map the interaction pattern: which screens exist, what connects them, what triggers each transition. This uses the inventory as its reference, so AI can build with vocabulary it already knows.
- 🎨 Visual: Dark prompt card on the left (screens / connects / triggers highlighted). Right: currently a placeholder panel — populate with a simple screen-flow diagram (matching the style of the Layer 2 slide) showing 2–3 connected screens when this slide is next revised.

---

### PROCESS — Step 6 of 6: Build one screen at a time
- Kicker: Step 6 of 6
- Title: Build one screen *at a time.*
- Prompt to use: "Build the [Screen name] screen. Goal: [what the user is trying to do here]. Layout: [how things should be arranged]. Content: [real field names and sample data, not lorem ipsum]. Audience: [who's using it]. Use the component inventory and interaction pattern above. Match component names exactly."
- Lead: Four ingredients, one complete brief: goal, layout, content, audience.
- Speaker notes: Step 6, the main event. Write build prompts with four ingredients: goal, layout, content, audience. A prompt with all four gives AI a complete brief instead of a fragment. If you have a reference screenshot, attach it before you prompt.
- 🎨 Visual: Dark prompt card on the left (Goal / Layout / Content / Audience highlighted in accent colours). Right: a browser-window mockup of a travel app screen ("Trip planning, finally together." with a temple photo background, a CTA button, and a bottom nav bar) — the finished-output example.
- ⚠️ Note for next revision: the lesson file's current version of this step adds a fifth ingredient, **flow context** (what screen this follows, what it leads to, what carries over), specifically to prevent screens losing continuity when built in isolation. This live deck was built before that change and still shows only 4 ingredients — flag for the next deck update so the prompt card matches the lesson file.

---

### STATEMENT — Refine each screen: three modes
- Kicker: After every build
- Title: Chat. Targeted feedback. *Direct edit.*
- 3-column comparison:
  - **Chat** — Structural changes. Use for broad changes, or anything that needs explanation. "Rearrange this screen so the primary action is in the top row." "Show me two alternative layouts."
  - **Targeted feedback** — One specific element. Name exactly what's wrong and where. "The button padding here is too tight." "Use the primary token colour on this card."
  - **Direct adjustment** — Quick nudges. Drag, resize, align directly, no prompt needed. Fast for spacing and alignment fixes you can just place by hand.
- Footer line (italic): Want a different direction without losing this version? Say so: "Save what we have and try a completely different layout."
- Speaker notes: After building, compare each screen to your design file and correct using whichever mode fits the size of the fix. Chat for structure. Targeted feedback for one specific element. Direct adjustment for quick nudges you can drag into place. Mixing all three based on the fix is faster than describing everything in chat.
- 🎨 Visual: Three equal-weight cards side by side, each with a coloured kicker label (Chat / Targeted feedback / Direct adjustment) and 1–2 example quotes.

---

### STATEMENT — What AI will and won't do
- Kicker: Set your expectations now
- Title: AI builds it. *You make it right.*
- 3 cards (third one highlighted solid purple):
  1. **Colours and type look generic at first** — …until your token file is applied. If something looks off-brand, check that the token step ran correctly.
  2. **Spacing and groupings need your eye** — AI places components correctly but doesn't always know what belongs together. That's design judgment, not a technical fix.
  3. **Each correction makes the next screen better** (highlighted) — The feedback loop is cumulative. A screen you correct well becomes the reference for everything that follows.
- Speaker notes: Set this expectation before students start. First screens will be technically assembled but rough. Spacing may be off, groupings may not match the design, hierarchy may feel flat. This is normal and documented — even with detailed prompts, AI output misses subtle but important details. The first output isn't the result, it's the starting point.
- 🎨 Visual: Three cards numbered 01/02/03 in large faint type, left two neutral/light, third card solid purple with white text to draw the eye to the cumulative-improvement point.

---

